1
00:00:00,550 --> 00:00:03,021
Most investment theses are about conviction.

2
00:00:03,041 --> 00:00:05,106
This one is built to be proven wrong.

3
00:00:07,306 --> 00:00:13,051
One command tests ten claims on public NEAR
data, each with a threshold fixed in advance.

4
00:00:13,071 --> 00:00:17,098
Five are supported, four are
refuted, one is inconclusive.

5
00:00:17,118 --> 00:00:20,355
Usage is far below last
year. We show that plainly.

6
00:00:22,455 --> 00:00:27,353
Verdicts and hashes of the exact data
and code live in a NEAR testnet contract.

7
00:00:27,373 --> 00:00:31,260
This command re-runs everything
offline and compares it with the chain.

8
00:00:31,280 --> 00:00:32,590
Everything matches.

9
00:00:32,610 --> 00:00:35,186
Change one byte, and it says mismatch.

10
00:00:36,586 --> 00:00:39,405
Here is the publishing
transaction on NEAR testnet.

11
00:00:41,805 --> 00:00:44,531
Each verdict also shows how fragile it is.

12
00:00:44,551 --> 00:00:48,752
Anyone can challenge it with other data,
and the contract recomputes the verdict.

13
00:00:48,772 --> 00:00:51,696
Our own test challenge flips revenue to refuted.

14
00:00:52,296 --> 00:00:56,335
Forecasts are pre-registered
on-chain, so they cannot be backdated.

15
00:00:56,355 --> 00:01:00,068
Evidence over conviction. Built
at NEAR Hackathon, testnet only.

